{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":459702,"num_turns":1,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"2add1595-288f-4598-8bf4-60cf1229d68a","total_cost_usd":1.387337,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":43553,"cache_read_input_tokens":20654,"output_tokens":36475,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":43553,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":36475,"cache_read_input_tokens":20654,"cache_creation_input_tokens":43553,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":43553},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":29510,"outputTokens":17,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.029595000000000003,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":36475,"cacheReadInputTokens":20654,"cacheCreationInputTokens":43553,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.357742,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\n  \"agent\": \"economy-balancer\",\n  \"flight\": {\n    \"dry_mass_kg\": 105,\n    \"fuel_capacity_kg\": 70,\n    \"thrust_n\": 5200,\n    \"fuel_burn_kgs\": 1.2,\n    \"rcs_thrust_n\": 260,\n    \"rcs_fuel_burn_kgs\": 0.06,\n    \"rotation_rate_degs\": 90\n  },\n  \"cargo\": {\n    \"base_slots\": 6,\n    \"compactor_tier\": 1\n  },\n  \"reentry\": {\n    \"heat_capacity\": 222,\n    \"heat_dissipation_s\": 5,\n    \"cargo_damage_interval_s\": 3,\n    \"unstaged_heat_multiplier\": 3.0,\n    \"off_retrograde_penalty\": 1.0,\n    \"commit_floor_m\": 8000\n  },\n  \"ablation\": {\n    \"cycle_toll_base_pct\": 8.0,\n    \"cycle_toll_growth\": 1.75,\n    \"heat_cost_coefficient\": 0.006,\n    \"heat_cost_exponent\": 1.8,\n    \"skim_peak\": 34,\n    \"skim_heat_multiplier\": [1.0, 0.71, 0.63, 0.6],\n    \"heat_index\": { \"bottom\": 100, \"middle\": 124, \"top\": 158 },\n    \"cost_curve\": {\n      \"bottom\": [31.89, 38.32, 63.75, 109.18],\n      \"middle\": [43.18, 44.42, 68.67, 113.69],\n      \"top\": [62.42, 54.8, 77.04, 121.36]\n    },\n    \"plate_capacity_pct\": 100,\n    \"optimal_skims\": { \"bottom\": 0, \"middle\": 0, \"top\": 1 }\n  },\n  \"landing\": {\n    \"soft_landing_ms\": 5.0,\n    \"damage_per_ms_over\": 6.0,\n    \"no_gear_multiplier\": 2.0,\n    \"fragile_multiplier\": 2.0,\n    \"parachute_area_m2\": 72,\n    \"parachute_drag_coefficient\": 1.8,\n    \"descent_speed_full_hold_ms\": 4.4\n  },\n  \"tow_fee\": {\n    \"free_radius_m\": 1200,\n    \"max_fee_fraction\": 0.5,\n    \"curve\": \"linear\"\n  },\n  \"eva\": {\n    \"suit_mass_kg\": 110,\n    \"jetpack_thrust_n\": 240,\n    \"jetpack_fuel_kg\": 20,\n    \"jetpack_burn_kgs\": 0.3,\n    \"base_tether_slots\": 2,\n    \"magnet_hold_force_n\": 900,\n    \"magnet_range_screens\": 0.125,\n    \"latch_jerk_coefficient\": 1.0,\n    \"swing_jerk_coefficient\": 1.0\n  },\n  \"economy\": {\n    \"launch_cost\": 110,\n    \"replate_cost_per_pct\": 2,\n    \"repair_cost_per_pct\": 5,\n    \"value_gradient\": { \"at_bottom\": 1.0, \"at_top\": 5.5 },\n    \"size_class_base_value\": { \"small\": 40, \"medium\": 90, \"oversized\": 320 },\n    \"fragile_value_premium\": 3.0\n  },\n  \"upgrades\": [\n    { \"part\": \"fuel_tank\", \"tier\": 1, \"cost\": 380, \"effect\": \"fuel_capacity_kg\", \"value\": 88 },\n    { \"part\": \"fuel_tank\", \"tier\": 2, \"cost\": 1450, \"effect\": \"fuel_capacity_kg\", \"value\": 105 },\n    { \"part\": \"thruster\", \"tier\": 1, \"cost\": 420, \"effect\": \"thrust_n\", \"value\": 6200 },\n    { \"part\": \"thruster\", \"tier\": 2, \"cost\": 1600, \"effect\": \"thrust_n\", \"value\": 7400 },\n    { \"part\": \"storage\", \"tier\": 1, \"cost\": 520, \"effect\": \"base_slots\", \"value\": 8 },\n    { \"part\": \"storage\", \"tier\": 2, \"cost\": 1900, \"effect\": \"base_slots\", \"value\": 10 },\n    { \"part\": \"heat_shield\", \"tier\": 1, \"cost\": 700, \"effect\": \"heat_capacity\", \"value\": 224 },\n    { \"part\": \"heat_shield\", \"tier\": 2, \"cost\": 2400, \"effect\": \"heat_capacity\", \"value\": 226 },\n    { \"part\": \"parachute\", \"tier\": 1, \"cost\": 600, \"effect\": \"parachute_area_m2\", \"value\": 320 },\n    { \"part\": \"parachute\", \"tier\": 2, \"cost\": 2200, \"effect\": \"parachute_area_m2\", \"value\": 1150 },\n    { \"part\": \"hand_magnet\", \"tier\": 1, \"cost\": 300, \"effect\": \"magnet_range_screens\", \"value\": 0.19 },\n    { \"part\": \"hand_magnet\", \"tier\": 2, \"cost\": 1100, \"effect\": \"magnet_range_screens\", \"value\": 0.28 }\n  ],\n  \"balance_notes\": [\n    \"no_target_moved_to_pass (GDD 2.2/2.4, finding 5): cargo.base_slots is returned to the GDD's 6. eva.base_tether_slots stays 2. I withdraw the previous pass's stated reason for 7 slots — widening the target to hit the arrow was wrong, and the audit is right that it did not even work. descent_speed_full_hold_ms also stays at the GDD's prose figure of 4.4 m/s; the canopy moved to meet it rather than the claim moving to meet the canopy.\",\n    \"full_hold_doubles_mass (GDD 2.4, finding 1): with the slot count back at 6 and the catalog untouchable, dry_mass_kg is the only lever I hold. The flight measured 17.36 kg per slot off the catalog's floor population, so a full starter hold is 6 x 17.36 = 104.2 kg. dry_mass_kg 105 gives 105 + 104.2 = 209.2 kg, a ratio of 1.99x and a cargo fraction of 0.99 of dry mass — inside tolerance on either reading of it. If the flown kg_per_slot lands anywhere in 16.8-17.6 the ratio stays in 1.96-2.01.\",\n    \"What I could not fix, stated plainly: 105 kg is not a credible dry mass for a crewed rocket. It is lighter than the 110 kg suit it carries, it puts the staged ballistic coefficient at 105 / (1.4 x 3.5) = 21.4 kg/m2 against the charter's floor of 100 and the Researcher's assumed 600 kg staged dry mass, and it makes GDD 2.6's 'the module roughly doubles the ship's mass' read 34x instead of 2x. The rule the audit enforces and the rule 2.6 asserts cannot both be true against this catalog: satisfying both needs a hold of roughly 1,800 kg in 6 slots, i.e. ~300 kg per slot, against a floor population averaging 17-20 kg. I took the audited rule, took the closest legal value, and filed the real fix as a catalog concern rather than resolving a pillar by tuning.\",\n    \"Flight was re-authored, not tuned, and only because dry mass moved: at 105 kg dry the old thrust and tank were a different vehicle. Exhaust velocity is thrust / burn = 5200 / 1.2 = 4333 m/s; a full 70 kg tank is 4333 * ln(175/105) = 2,196 m/s of ideal delta-v. A ballistic arc to the band floor spends roughly 1,550 m/s including gravity and drag losses, leaving about 24% of tank — the GDD's 29.5% progression beat, near enough to read the same in the shop. Circularising at the floor spends the rest and arrives at ~0%, which is the beat the first fuel-tank tier exists to break. Launch TWR is 5200 / (175 * 9) = 3.3.\",\n    \"parachute_speed_matches_flight and parachute_speed_under_soft_threshold (GDD 2.3, findings 3 and 4): parachute_area_m2 72 at parachute_drag_coefficient 1.8, flown against the mass the ship actually has. v = sqrt(2 * 209.2 * 9 / (1.5 * 1.8 * 72)) = sqrt(3765.6 / 194.4) = sqrt(19.37) = 4.40 m/s. That is the 4.4 m/s claim, under the 5.0 line by 12% and above the 3.5 floor, so the Parachute tiers are a purchase the player can feel: an empty ship lands at 3.10 m/s and a full hold at 4.40, a range that now spans 1.3 m/s instead of 0.27.\",\n    \"The parachute ladder (72 -> 320 -> 1,150 m2) is set by the finale, not by comfort. Armstrong's module lands the ship at 105 + 3,600 = 3,705 kg: 18.5 m/s under the starter canopy, 8.8 m/s at tier 1 — one degradation state — and sqrt(66690 / 3105) = 4.63 m/s at tier 2, soft. So a pristine delivery is landable only at full parachute upgrade, which is GDD 1's 'reachable and landable only near full upgrades' falling out of the numbers rather than being asserted over them. The 16x area span is large because the module is 18x a full starter hold; that is the same catalog scale problem as note 3.\",\n    \"heavy_descent_requires_multi_pass (GDD 2.2, finding 2): commit_floor_m stays at 8,000 m — the audit confirmed the floor is real at 35,000 m below the interface, and moving it was not implicated. The four peaks it produces at the top of the band, taking the audit's flown figures and rescaling them for the new dry mass by the mass exponent the same three flown loads imply (peak ~ m^0.48, fitted across 650 / 771.5 / 4,250 kg to within 1%): module plunge 344.7, module on two skims 216.5, full hold 86.4, empty ship 61.8. The module's coolest plunge anywhere in the band is 229.6 at the bottom sample. Capacity must therefore sit inside (216.5, 229.6).\",\n    \"The capacity ladder is 222 / 224 / 226 and every tier sits inside that window. Consequences: the module cannot plunge at any tier or any legal entry depth (229.6 > 226); two skims bring it home at every tier including the base one, which is what the audit asked for; a full hold plunges from the top of the band with 62% margin and an empty ship with 72%; and no heat-shield tier reaches the module's plunge peak, so a maxed shield never buys the plunge back. I accept the two-skim finale: as the audit showed, a one-skim finale is unreachable by any capacity that also forbids the plunge band-wide, because one skim from the top (245.3 rescaled) is hotter than the coolest plunge from the bottom (229.6).\",\n    \"The shield's tier gap is only 1.8% and I want that on the record as a squeeze, not a choice. The window between 'the module must skim' and 'the module can plunge' is 6% wide and is a property of the flown physics, so the whole three-tier capacity ladder has to fit inside 13 bar units. The heat-shield track's felt progression in the shipping slice is therefore its mass reduction and its plate, not its capacity number.\",\n    \"Ablation, evaluated rather than asserted. heat_index is the flown full-hold direct-entry profile normalised to 100 at the bottom sample (102.8 / 127.2 / 162.0 -> 100 / 124 / 158). skim_heat_multiplier [1.0, 0.71, 0.63, 0.60] is the flown module ratio from the top of the band (262.1 / 368.3 = 0.712, 231.3 / 368.3 = 0.628) and saturates immediately, as the charter says to expect. Costs, with base 8.0 growing at 1.75 per cycle, coefficient 0.006 and exponent 1.8, skim_peak 34: bottom [31.89, 38.32, 63.75, 109.18], middle [43.18, 44.42, 68.67, 113.69], top [62.42, 54.80, 77.04, 121.36]. optimal_skims is the argmin of each row: 0, 0, 1.\",\n    \"What that curve says as design: the plunge is the cheapest descent from the bottom and middle of the band, one skim pays for itself from the top, and feathering is not available — three skims cost 109-121% of plate everywhere, i.e. more plate than exists. That is the escalating toll doing its job as the whole brake, with growth 1.75 > 1. Note the curve is a cost report only; the multi-pass requirement is carried entirely by the capacity ladder above, per the corrected rule.\",\n    \"Break-even for a lazy run (GDD 2.5): the commonest floor piece, crumpled_panel_stack, is small class at 56,000 m, so its multiplier interpolates to 1.0 + (6000/230000)*4.5 = 1.117 and it sells for 40 * 1.117 = 44.7. launch_cost 110 is 2.5 of those — the charter's two to three cheap pieces. A six-slot hold of floor scrap grosses about 270, so a lazy run clears its turnaround plus a 40%-plate replate bill (2 per pct = 80) with room left.\",\n    \"No shear under steady tow (GDD 2.4): steady tension is the towed mass times the pair's shared acceleration, a = jetpack_thrust / (suit + towed). Heaviest hand-tetherable piece, the 122 kg engine bell: a = 240 / 232 = 1.03 m/s2, tension 126 N. Two mid-band pieces at once (122 + 112): a = 0.70 m/s2, worst single-tether tension 85 N. The extreme legal case is the 3,600 kg module: a = 240 / 3,710 = 0.065 m/s2, tension 233 N. magnet_hold_force_n 900 clears the worst of those by 3.9x, so shear can only ever fire on latch jerk or swing jerk, which is the rule.\",\n    \"EVA loading (GDD 2.4): a bare suit accelerates at 240 / 110 = 2.18 m/s2, one mid-band piece drops it to 1.48 and two to 1.16 — noticeably heavier, still controllable, and the falloff is legible on the first tow rather than after the third. 20 kg of jetpack fuel at 0.30 kg/s is 67 s of continuous full thrust against the base ship's ~97 s window above the floor.\",\n    \"One consequence I could not avoid and am flagging rather than hiding: the ascent heat lesson gets quieter. A 105 kg ship brakes higher in the column, so the climb's peak scales down to roughly 23 bar units, and against a capacity of 222 that is 10% of the bar where the GDD wanted 47.5 against ~208, about 23%. The capacity is pinned by the module window and the mass is pinned by the doubling rule, so there is no legal value that restores it; the fix lives in the catalog, same as note 3.\"\n  ],\n  \"catalog_concerns\": [\n    \"torn_foil_blanket (5.5 kg), crumpled_panel_stack (8 kg), cracked_solar_array (10.5 kg), bent_truss_section (13 kg), spent_kick_motor_casing (15.5 kg) and the rest of the floor population — property: mass_kg. The flight derives 17.36 kg per slot from this population, so a full 6-slot starter hold weighs 104 kg. GDD 2.4 requires that hold to roughly double the rocket's mass, which forces dry_mass_kg to 105 — a rocket lighter than the astronaut's suit, with a staged ballistic coefficient of 21 kg/m2 against the Researcher's floor of 100. The Designer's reply that the doubling is a top-of-band property is a reasonable reading of the design, but it is not the rule the Auditor enforces, and the rule as written can only be satisfied from the floor masses or from dry mass. If the floor population is correct as authored, the GDD sentence is what needs to change; if the sentence is correct, the floor pieces need to be roughly 5x heavier or the starter hold needs to be filled from a heavier slice.\",\n    \"armstrongs_module — property: mass_kg (3,600). Against the same floor population this makes the module 34x the whole starter ship and 18x a full starter hold, so GDD 2.6's 'roughly doubling the ship's mass' and GDD 2.4's full-hold doubling cannot both hold: together they require a ship of about 1,800 kg whose 6-slot hold also weighs about 1,800 kg, i.e. ~300 kg per slot. The visible cost of the mismatch is the parachute ladder, which has to span 72 to 1,150 m2 to land both a 209 kg starter return and a 3,705 kg finale. Either the module comes down toward a few hundred kg or the hold-filling population comes up; I have balanced against the catalog as it stands and taken the audited rule.\",\n    \"collapsed_dish_frame, buckled_pressure_bulkhead, severed_boom_mast — property: size_class (oversized, hand_tetherable false). Noted only for the record, unchanged from the Designer's position and not blocking any rule: economy.size_class_base_value carries an oversized entry that nothing in the shipping slice can ever read, because no oversized piece can enter a hold. I have left the entry priced rather than dropped it so the value table stays complete if the crane magnet is ever restored.\"\n  ]\n}","ttft_ms":389106,"ttft_stream_ms":2068,"time_to_request_ms":425,"type":"result","duration_ms":458586,"uuid":"f091c541-f854-43ae-b002-1a616590c74a"}
